<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yury of Moscow</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1281">1281</a>–<a href="/years/1325">1325</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Yury Danilovich, Yuri Danilovich, Yuri of Moscow · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rurik-dynasty">Rurik dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yury of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> was prince of Moscow from 1303 and the first Moscow prince to hold the grand princely throne of Vladimir. Married to Konchaka, a sister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbek-khan">Uzbek Khan</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a>, he obtained the khan's patent in 1317 and fought the princes of Tver, whose ruler Mikhail was executed at the Horde in 1318. He was himself killed at the Horde in 1325 by Mikhail's son Dmitry.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Yury Danilovich was the eldest son of Daniel of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, the first prince of Moscow. His first military action was the defence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pereslavl-zalessky">Pereslavl-Zalessky</a> against Andrey of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gorodets">Gorodets</a>, grand prince of Vladimir. Because Daniel had never held the grand princely title, his descendants had no legitimate claim to Vladimir under the traditional rules of succession. When Andrey died on 27 July 1304, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokhta">Tokhta</a>, khan of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a>, therefore gave the title to Yury's uncle, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mikhail-of-tver">Mikhail of Tver</a>. Yury rebelled, and two campaigns by Mikhail against Moscow, in 1305 and 1308, forced him to accept the khan's decision.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Yury meanwhile arranged the murder of Prince Konstantin of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ryazan">Ryazan</a>, whom his father had captured in 1302 and held in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, and annexed the Ryazan fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolomna">Kolomna</a>. He also took Mozhaisk, which had belonged to the princes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smolensk">Smolensk</a>. By 1314 he had the backing of Metropolitan Peter and a military alliance with Novgorod against Tver.</p><p>In 1315 he went to the Horde, where he spent two years. His bride Konchaka converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox Christianity</a> and was baptised Agafiia. Returning with a large force of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatars</a>, he approached Tver, but his army was defeated and his wife and his brother Boris were captured; he fled to Novgorod and sued for peace. When his wife died unexpectedly as a hostage in Tver, he told the khan that Mikhail had ordered her poisoned. Both princes were summoned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarai">Sarai</a>, and after a trial Mikhail was executed on 22 November 1318.</p><p>As grand prince from 1318 to 1322 Yury was resisted by other princes and by the people, who thought his appointment illegitimate. He was charged with gathering the tribute for the Horde, and in his last two years in the office the khan sent no fewer than four punitive expeditions to the northeastern principalities to enforce it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1322 Dmitry of Tver persuaded the khan at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarai">Sarai</a> that Yury had kept a large part of the tribute, and received the patent for Vladimir himself. Yury then led the army of Novgorod against the Swedes, founded a fort at the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neva-river">Neva River</a>, signed the Treaty of Orekhovo in 1323 and conquered Velikiy Ustyug the same year. Summoned to the Horde for trial, he was killed by Dmitry on 21 November 1325 before any formal investigation. Dmitry was executed at the Horde eight months later. Early in 1326 Yury's remains were brought to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> and buried by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> under Metropolitan Peter.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Yury of Moscow” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Sarai. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p></section>
